{"id":1784,"date":"2020-12-07T15:35:55","date_gmt":"2020-12-07T23:35:55","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=1784"},"modified":"2024-08-27T13:07:58","modified_gmt":"2024-08-27T20:07:58","slug":"codrone-blockly-sr-remote-control","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/codrone-blockly-sr-remote-control\/","title":{"rendered":"Remote Control"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215643c06a\" name=\"6a2215643c06a\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Keyboard input<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">You may be wondering how to use the keyboard to control CoDrone. In the first activity, you will learn how to<\/span> <span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">use your spacebar to turn on your CoDrone LEDs. In Junior mode, you can select a color. In Senior mode, you<\/span> <span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">will be using the RGB values of your color.<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">First, turn off your CoDrone LEDs at the start of your program. You can do this by setting the brightness of<\/span> <span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">the color of your choice to the lowest value, 1.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1785&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215643c182\" name=\"6a2215643c182\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>While loop<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Since you want your program to constantly check if you are pressing a key, you will need to implement a<\/span> <span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">forever loop. For this example, use a repeat loop block and connect an &#8220;is_code_running()&#8221; block from the<\/span> orange s<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">tatus checkers menu.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1786&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215643c1dc\" name=\"6a2215643c1dc\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Conditional<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Now it&#8217;s time to check for keyboard input! Remember that when a program needs to make a decision you will use a conditional statement. Go to the logic menu and grab a conditional block. In the keyboard menu, grab the &#8220;When backspace key press&#8221; that has the attachment on the side. From the drop-down menu, select &#8220;spacebar&#8221;. <\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1787&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">If the spacebar is pressed, you want your CoDrone LEDs to turn on. Else, turn them off by resetting the brightness back to 1. <\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1788&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215643c22a\" name=\"6a2215643c22a\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Wait block<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">You will need to add a wait block after the conditional statement. This is because if you press and hold the<\/span> <span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">spacebar, you will be sending multiple messages to CoDrone. A delay of 500 milliseconds will be enough to<\/span> <span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">prevent CoDrone from disconnecting.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1789&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215643c2d2\" name=\"6a2215643c2d2\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>More keys<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">If you want to check for multiple key presses, you will need to include an else if statement for every extra key press.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1790&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1791&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215643c382\" name=\"6a2215643c382\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Adding flight commands<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Now that you have the basic idea, instead of changing LEDs, you will be adding flight commands! First, you<\/span> <span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">will set up the structure of the program to handle roll, pitch, yaw, and throttle. Since there are positive and<\/span> <span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">negative values for each flight direction, there are 8 total possible commands plus the spacebar for<\/span> the\u00a0<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">emergency stop.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">In this example, the following keyboard commands will be used, but you can change the values to whatever<\/span> <span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">you want.<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>W\/S- <\/b><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Positive\/Negative throttle<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">\u00a0<\/span><\/li>\n<li><b>D\/A- <\/b><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Positive\/Negative Yaw<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">\u00a0<\/span><\/li>\n<li><b>Up\/Down- <\/b><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Positive\/Negative Pitch<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">\u00a0<\/span><\/li>\n<li><b>Right\/Left- <\/b><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Positive\/Negative<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">\u00a0<\/span><\/li>\n<li><b>Space- <\/b><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Emergency stop<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">\u00a0<\/span><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Think about how you should program your if statements. Remember that if and else-if are useful when you<\/span> <span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">only want to execute one of those commands. However, using one if statement and 7 if-else statements<\/span> <span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">means you can only fly in one direction at a time! To be able to fly in more than one direction, you need to<\/span> <span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">group your if statements into roll, pitch, yaw, and throttle. For example:<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">If W is pressed, set Throttle to 75 2\u00a0\r\n\r\nElse if S is pressed, set throttle to -75 3\r\n\r\nElse, set throttle to 0.<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The else statement is very important. It resets the value of throttle to zero whenever neither key is pressed. <\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1792&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1834&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215643c3d9\" name=\"6a2215643c3d9\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<b>Activity: <\/b><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Obstacle course\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Now that you have your new flight controller, build an obstacle course and try to fly to the finish line without hitting anything! If you need to, adjust the values of the delays and add more keys for more functionality. <\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 8 Keyboard input [vc_column_text]You may be wondering how to use the keyboard to control CoDrone. In the first activity, you will learn how to use your spacebar to&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/codrone-blockly-sr-remote-control\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":1753,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":806,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1814,1760,1817,1763,1777,1737,1815,1793,626,1533,1438,853,144,167,171,63],"lesson-tag":[2108,2164,2121,2122,2111,2126],"class_list":["post-1784","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-blockly","lesson-tag-codrone","lesson-tag-keyboard","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-remote-control","lesson-tag-senior","module-senior","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1784","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":8,"href":"https:\/\/